# Mechanical UI/UX Enforcement Contract

> [!summary]
> Scripts prove that required evidence exists and that objective constraints pass. They do not decide whether a design is beautiful. A critic and judge handle contextual quality only after hard evidence gates pass.

## Activation

Activate this contract for user-visible changes. Select a proportional lane:

| Lane | Typical scope | Minimum evidence |
|---|---|---|
| Lite | isolated copy, icon, token, or small component change | touched state, impacted viewport, static checks, focused screenshot |
| Standard | component behavior, tab, modal, page section, responsive change | relevant roles, states, data extremes, compact and full-width viewports, focused flow, accessibility and geometry checks |
| Premium | route-wide redesign, complex workflow, high-visibility surface | baseline matrix, design alternatives when needed, human direction, all relevant states/viewports, independent critic and judge, performance check, bounded repair |

Documentation-only changes do not activate browser QA.

## Required run contract

Each run must declare:

- workflow and project IDs;
- task and acceptance criteria;
- route or surface scope;
- affected roles and permissions;
- required viewports or window classes;
- UI states and tabs;
- overlays and their triggers;
- data fixtures, including empty, normal, dense, long-text, and error cases when relevant;
- target user journey;
- selected DNA and surface-recipe references;
- objective validators;
- critic rubric;
- evidence paths and freshness rules;
- repair-attempt budget;
- human approval requirements.

Unknown requirements return INVALID_CONTRACT or HUMAN_DECISION_REQUIRED. They are not silently omitted.

## State and evidence matrix

Every evidence item binds to a tuple:

route × role × viewport × data fixture × UI state × overlay × revision

A route screenshot without this metadata cannot prove full coverage.

Common states to consider:

- initial/default;
- loading and refreshing;
- empty;
- populated;
- dense or maximum practical data;
- long labels and wrapped content;
- error and retry;
- disabled and permission-limited;
- active, selected, expanded, sorted, and filtered;
- success confirmation;
- destructive confirmation;
- every relevant tab, drawer, dropdown, tooltip, popover, and modal;
- keyboard focus and reduced-motion behavior;
- narrow, intermediate, and full-width layouts.

The matrix is impact-derived. Do not generate every Cartesian combination when a state is impossible or irrelevant; record the exclusion and reason.

## Hard blockers

The following cannot be offset by a weighted visual score:

- stale or unreachable primary action;
- broken target journey;
- wrong role or permission behavior;
- overlap that hides or prevents interaction;
- clipped critical content;
- uncontrolled horizontal overflow;
- off-screen or unreachable controls;
- modal background remains interactive;
- missing modal focus entry, containment, Escape behavior, close control, or focus return;
- ambiguous or missing scroll owner;
- required state or viewport evidence missing;
- uncaught page error, critical console error, or failed required request;
- inaccessible name/role/value or keyboard trap;
- destructive action without required protection;
- evidence captured against the wrong revision or stale runtime;
- validator, fixture, or acceptance contract invalid.

A hard blocker yields REPAIR_REQUIRED, BLOCKED, or INVALID_CONTRACT—never PASS.

## Geometry and scroll checks

Browser evidence collection should inspect relevant visible elements for:

- bounding-box intersection beyond allowed overlays;
- clipping against viewport and scroll containers;
- unexpected horizontal overflow;
- zero-sized interactive elements;
- fixed/sticky elements covering content;
- modal height against viewport;
- exactly one intended vertical scroll owner for a modal body;
- visible close and primary actions at supported sizes;
- touch target dimensions where applicable;
- layout shifts during loading and interaction.

Geometry findings must reference selectors, bounding boxes, viewport, screenshot, and state.

## Modal contract

For each affected modal:

1. Trigger is visible and usable.
2. Dialog has an accessible name.
3. Background is inert while open.
4. Initial focus is intentional; large semantic content may focus a static heading or introduction.
5. Tab and Shift+Tab remain within the dialog.
6. Escape closes unless an explicitly justified critical workflow prevents it.
7. A visible close or cancel control exists.
8. Closing returns focus to the invoker or a documented logical destination.
9. Dense content has a bounded body and explicit internal scroll owner.
10. Header and actions remain understandable while body content scrolls.
11. Narrow and full-width viewports remain usable.

## Dashboard and analytics contract

Classify the surface:

- Presentation dashboard: communicates current status and priority.
- Exploration dashboard: supports filtering, comparison, drill-down, and discovery.

Checks include:

- page purpose and most important metric are evident;
- filters have visible applied state and affect every promised section;
- linked views remain synchronized;
- chart encodings and color meanings stay consistent;
- empty and no-result states explain the active filter context;
- annotations add meaning without covering data;
- information density preserves scanability;
- generated insight, loading, failure, and stale-data states are distinct;
- primary and secondary actions remain visually ordered;
- long ranges, extreme values, and narrow widths do not break layout.

## Accessibility and performance

Accessibility gates use WCAG 2.2 as the current baseline and combine automated checks with keyboard and human inspection where automation is insufficient.

Performance evidence should include relevant lab signals:

- loading performance;
- interaction responsiveness or a documented lab proxy;
- visual stability;
- route-level bundle or request regressions when the change could affect them.

Performance budgets are project- and surface-specific. Core Web Vitals thresholds are useful anchors, but local lab data does not prove field performance.

## Critic contract

Each finding contains:

- finding and criterion IDs;
- exact observation;
- route/state/viewport/data context;
- evidence reference;
- user impact;
- severity and rationale;
- project-DNA or external anchor;
- smallest repair direction;
- verification method;
- uncertainty and alternative valid directions.

The critic must:

- search for counterexamples;
- call out missing evidence;
- separate objective defects from aesthetic preference;
- avoid “make it modern,” “looks off,” or unsupported PASS statements;
- normalize duplicate symptoms that share one cause.

## Judge contract

The judge runs only after deterministic gates pass or have an explicit non-waivable failure result.

The judge evaluates contextual dimensions selected for the surface, such as:

- user-job clarity;
- information hierarchy;
- interaction quality;
- responsive composition;
- consistency with project DNA;
- data legibility;
- visual character and restraint;
- domain credibility.

Weights can prioritize dimensions, but each critical dimension also has a minimum. A high aggregate cannot hide a failed critical dimension. No universal “premium score” exists.

## Bounded repair loop

Default maximum: three candidate repair rounds for the same contract.

- Objective failure: repair the smallest cause and rerun affected evidence.
- Missing evidence: collect it; do not redesign.
- Subjective ambiguity: stop for human choice.
- Repeated identical failure: escalate with the trace and suspected harness gap.
- Contract change: requires an explicit decision; repairers cannot weaken criteria.

## Validator states

- PASS — all required objective evidence is current and accepted judgment is recorded.
- REPAIR_REQUIRED — an in-scope objective defect has a bounded repair path.
- BLOCKED — a runtime, dependency, or authority condition prevents proof.
- HUMAN_DECISION_REQUIRED — multiple valid directions or a material tradeoff needs approval.
- INVALID_CONTRACT — required scope, criteria, evidence bindings, or validator ownership is missing or contradictory.

## Knowledge writeback

Publish only:

- a reusable pattern;
- a corrected bad rule;
- a new project-DNA decision;
- a repeatable browser/runtime failure and fix;
- a new fixture or validator requirement;
- a known exception with owner and review date.

Do not publish every finding, screenshot, or CSS adjustment as permanent knowledge.

## Implementation gap

This contract is now specified and linked to the existing Broskie architecture. The remaining engineering work is to wire schemas, Playwright evidence collectors, geometry checks, and validators into the runtime orchestrator, then prove them on one project-agnostic vertical slice before broad adoption.
